  "image_name": "4f7580a6b127befdd8998cb6a7c8453c.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes hormone and cytokine signaling across three bone-related cell types—macrophages, osteoclasts, and osteoblasts—and how these pathways appear to coordinate inflammation, bone resorption, and bone formation. A macrophage at the upper left expresses receptors including TSHR and FSHR, which feed into regulation of the inflammatory gene Tnfa. The macrophage also releases cytokines such as IL-6, TNF, and IL-1β toward the osteoclast.\n\nThe osteoclast is shown as a signaling hub for bone resorption. It carries several receptors, including AVPR1, OXTR, GHR, and IGFR, and downstream pathways like NF-κB, JNK, and MAPK converge on Tnfa-related responses. A TSHβ variant produced by macrophages seems to act on both osteoclasts and osteoblasts. Osteoclasts also generate osteoprotegerin, which is depicted inhibiting RANK signaling, highlighting control of osteoclast activity.\n\nOn the osteoblast side, signaling is linked to bone-building genes. WNT5a activates Frizzled/LRP5/6 and PKCδ, and this pathway feeds into expression of markers such as Vegf, Opn, and Col1a1. Additional receptors, including MC2R and others, modulate osteoblast behavior, and a mesenchymal stem cell above appears to contribute signals into the osteoblast lineage. Overall, the figure depicts a network balancing inflammatory and anabolic bone pathways.",
